# TLS mode: CloudflareOrigin
# Requires: Cloudflare DNS for mobaide.com set to "Proxied" (orange cloud),
#           SSL/TLS mode set to "Full (strict)" in the Cloudflare dashboard,
#           a free 15-year Origin Certificate generated under
#             SSL/TLS > Origin Server > Create Certificate
#           scoped to EXACTLY the proxied hosts — mobaide.com,
#           www.mobaide.com, api.mobaide.com — with NO wildcard, and saved
#           on the VPS as:
#             /opt/mobaide/tls/origin-cert.pem
#             /opt/mobaide/tls/origin-key.pem
#           (both chmod 600, owner matches the user running docker compose).
#           EXCEPTION: app.mobaide.com stays "DNS only" (gray cloud) with an
#           ACME HTTP-01 cert — see the comment on its site block below.
#           The no-wildcard requirement is what makes that exception work: a
#           SAN covering app.mobaide.com silently disables ACME for it.

# Cloudflare fronts the proxied hosts, so the peer Caddy sees is a Cloudflare
# edge, not the visitor. Without trusting Cloudflare's published ranges
# everything behind the orange cloud — per-IP rate limits, access logs, and
# the ad-consent network fingerprint that has to match the gray-cloud
# app.mobaide.com sign-up — sees a Cloudflare address.
#
# The visitor's address comes from Cf-Connecting-Ip, NOT X-Forwarded-For.
# Cloudflare appends to any X-Forwarded-For the client sent, so its leftmost
# entry is whatever the client wrote — and uvicorn (FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS=*)
# reads exactly that entry. Cf-Connecting-Ip is one address that Cloudflare
# always overwrites. Do not add X-Forwarded-For to client_ip_headers as a
# fallback: that reopens the spoof. Every reverse_proxy below then replaces
# X-Forwarded-For with the single resolved {client_ip}.
#
# Global options must be the first block. Ranges from
# https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and /ips-v6 (checked 2026-09-02); the
# LetsEncrypt mode has no proxy in front and needs none of this.
{
    servers {
        client_ip_headers Cf-Connecting-Ip
        trusted_proxies static 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 2400:cb00::/32 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 2405:8100::/32 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
    }
}

# Send www to the apex. Without this block Caddy matches no site for the www
# host and answers its default empty 200, which is what www.mobaide.com did
# before: a bare 200 with a zero-byte body and no `Via: 1.1 Caddy`, because the
# request reached Caddy but never reached a reverse_proxy.
#
# 308 rather than 301 so the method and body survive the redirect.
#
# The same Cloudflare Origin Certificate serves this host, so www must be one
# of its SANs. List it explicitly when issuing the cert; do NOT reach for the
# wildcard the dashboard offers, which would also cover app.mobaide.com and
# break it (see the app site block below). Also needs a proxied DNS record.

# HSTS — the `header Strict-Transport-Security` line in both site blocks below.
#
# It lives here, not in the app: central-server is reached over plain HTTP on
# the compose network, and browsers ignore Strict-Transport-Security on a
# non-HTTPS response. Caddy terminates TLS, so this is the only layer that can
# emit it truthfully.
#
# max-age=300 is the FIRST RUNG of a deliberate ratchet, not the target value.
# HSTS is effectively irreversible: once a browser has cached a max-age,
# lowering it only reaches clients that come back before the old one expires, so
# the short rung proves the header is emitted correctly without committing
# anyone to a year. No includeSubDomains (it would bind api.mobaide.com,
# get.mobaide.com on GitHub Pages, and every future subdomain) and no preload.
# The full ladder and the audit it requires are in docs/deployment.md and
# tracked in CLA-172.
#
# In this TLS mode Cloudflare also sits in front and can be configured to add
# its own HSTS. Two sources for one header is a way to lose track of what is
# actually pinned — leave Cloudflare's HSTS off and keep this file authoritative.
#
# Each site block, including the (app_mobaide) snippet below, carries its own
# copy of the header rather than inheriting it from a shared block.

# app.mobaide.com — the browser SPA (web/), served same-origin with the relay
# so CORS never enters the picture. This snippet MUST stay byte-identical in
# Caddyfile.LetsEncrypt and Caddyfile.CloudflareOrigin: the deploy copies
# exactly one of these files to the server whole, so there is no cross-file
# include to share it.
(app_mobaide) {
    header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"
    encode zstd gzip

    # Backend prefixes — same upstream as the apex/api block. Everything the
    # SPA talks to is same-origin: fetch to /api, WebSockets to /ws and
    # /relay, and the /proxy HTTP+WS agent passthrough. The list mirrors
    # web/vite.config.ts's dev proxy — change both together.
    @backend path /api /api/* /ws /ws/* /relay /relay/* /proxy /proxy/*
    handle @backend {
        reverse_proxy central-server:9000 {
            # Exactly one address, so uvicorn's leftmost-entry read cannot
            # pick up a value the client wrote — see the apex block.
            header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}

            # On a graceful `caddy reload`, keep already-established proxied
            # streams (terminal WebSockets, agent tunnels) alive under the
            # old config for up to this long instead of force-closing them
            # the moment the config swaps. Only reloads benefit — container
            # restarts still drop everything.
            stream_close_delay 5m
        }
    }

    # Everything else is the SPA: static files from the current release
    # symlink, unknown paths rewritten to index.html for client-side routing.
    handle {
        root * /srv/www/current

        # Static files never pass through central_server's security-header
        # middleware, so this block carries its own copies of the universal
        # headers plus the SPA CSP. The script-src hash pins the pre-paint
        # theme-boot inline <script> in web/index.html; editing that script
        # without updating the hash in BOTH Caddyfiles silently disables it
        # (theme flash, nothing worse) — deploy_web.py preflights the match.
        #
        # The accounts.google.com entries are what Google Identity Services
        # needs for the sign-in button. This is now the only surface in the
        # deployment that concedes them — the server-rendered sign-in pages are
        # gone, so security_headers.py names no third-party origin at all. The
        # style-src 'unsafe-inline' is not optional: the GIS client appends
        # an inline <style> to <head> as it loads, and renderButton({width})
        # emits an inline style= attribute. img-src adds only data: and
        # blob: — the chat view draws agent images from data: URLs and
        # attachment previews from blob: URLs, both made in the page; the
        # sign-in button and its Google logo render inside a cross-origin
        # iframe, so they are governed by Google's policy, not ours.
        #
        # The appleid.cdn-apple.com entry is the Sign in with Apple JS
        # bundle, and it is the whole cost of that provider: under usePopup
        # the SDK calls window.open (governed by no CSP directive at all) and
        # reads the answer off a postMessage listener, so appleid.apple.com
        # itself needs no frame-src and no connect-src. The locale segment in
        # the path is load-bearing — it must match web/src/auth/appleSignIn.ts.
        #
        # Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy has to stay same-origin-allow-popups:
        # same-origin severs the opener that BOTH sign-in popups post their
        # credential back through. Permissions-Policy carries the FedCM
        # allowance this origin needs and security_headers.py no longer grants
        # anywhere:
        # the GIS button frame carries allow="identity-credentials-get", and a
        # frame's allow only delegates what the embedding document grants to
        # that origin, so identity-credentials-get=(self) would refuse it and
        # take sign-in down. Apple's SDK uses no FedCM, so it adds nothing
        # here. Both are unconditional, for the same reason the CSP's
        # third-party origins are: Caddy cannot read the .env, and neither
        # button mounts when GET /api/version reports no client id for it.
        #
        # microphone=(self) is voice input: the SPA records in the browser and
        # streams the audio to the user's own agent (the AudioWorklet module it
        # loads is a same-origin static file, so script-src needs nothing
        # new). Only this origin gets it; central_server's pages keep
        # microphone=(). Changing this header takes a Caddy reload.
        header {
            Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-DQjnNfb9BFsBDwtrOfOC9aSi5NaqDDr+KkPgo0PMtbY=' https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client https://appleid.cdn-apple.com/appleauth/static/jsapi/appleid/1/en_US/appleid.auth.js; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://accounts.google.com/gsi/style; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self' https://accounts.google.com/gsi/; frame-src https://accounts.google.com/gsi/; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
            Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin-allow-popups"
            X-Frame-Options "DENY"
            X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
            Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
            Permissions-Policy `camera=(), microphone=(self), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), identity-credentials-get=(self "https://accounts.google.com")`
        }

        # route {} forces listed order: the cache headers must run AFTER the
        # try_files rewrite so the /assets/* matcher sees the final path.
        route {
            try_files {path} /index.html
            # index.html and unhashed public/ files (wordmark.png, the icons)
            # must revalidate on every load so a deploy is visible immediately
            # (file_server's ETag/Last-Modified make that a cheap 304);
            # Vite's content-hashed /assets/* are immutable by construction.
            header Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
            header /assets/* Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
            file_server
        }
    }
}

www.mobaide.com {
    tls /etc/caddy/tls/origin-cert.pem /etc/caddy/tls/origin-key.pem
    header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"
    redir https://mobaide.com{uri} 308
}

mobaide.com, api.mobaide.com {
    tls /etc/caddy/tls/origin-cert.pem /etc/caddy/tls/origin-key.pem
    header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"
    encode zstd gzip
    reverse_proxy central-server:9000 {
        # Replace, never append: the relay keys every per-IP rate limit on
        # the leftmost X-Forwarded-For entry, so the header must carry only
        # the client address Caddy resolved (the peer, or Cf-Connecting-Ip
        # from a trusted Cloudflare edge). Caddy's default keeps whatever a
        # trusted proxy forwarded, client-written entries included.
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}

        # Same rationale as in (app_mobaide): every deploy now runs
        # `caddy reload`, and without this delay a reload would force-close
        # every established native-client WebSocket (agent tunnels, Android/
        # iOS relay sockets) the moment the config swaps.
        stream_close_delay 5m
    }
}

# The SPA host. DELIBERATELY no `tls` directive and DELIBERATELY gray-cloud
# (DNS only) even though the rest of this mode is proxied: orange cloud would
# put Cloudflare's ~100 s proxy idle timeout in the path of every relay
# WebSocket, and a gray-cloud host cannot serve the Cloudflare Origin cert
# (browsers do not trust the Origin CA). Caddy ACME-issues a Let's Encrypt
# certificate for this one host over HTTP-01 instead — port 80 is already
# open. If Cloudflare fronting is ever required here, orange cloud + the
# origin cert does work: the app-level ws_ping_interval (20 s) beats the
# 100 s idle timeout.
#
# CONSTRAINT: that ACME issuance happens only if NO loaded certificate matches
# this host — i.e. only if the origin cert above is not a wildcard. Caddy's
# certificate cache is global, not per-site, so a `*.mobaide.com` SAN loaded
# by the blocks above matches SNI app.mobaide.com and disables automatic HTTPS
# here. The only symptom is a log line:
#   "skipping automatic certificate management because one or more matching
#    certificates are already loaded","domain":"app.mobaide.com"
# after which this host serves the Origin CA cert and every browser rejects
# it. The override (`auto_https ignore_loaded_certs`) is server-scoped, so it
# would force ACME for the orange-clouded hosts too and is not usable here —
# issue the origin cert without a wildcard instead. deploy_central.py's
# preflight checks this and warns.
app.mobaide.com {
    import app_mobaide
}
